# =============================================================================
# Stationeers TestRig - SESSION LOCK (single source of truth)
# =============================================================================
# This .template file is committed and is the authoritative description of the
# session-lock mechanism. The ACTIVE lock is the sibling file `session.lock`
# (gitignored), created and maintained by either launcher through the shared
# implementation in `TestRig/rig-lock.ps1`. Every other reference (root
# CLAUDE.md, TestRig/CLAUDE.md, TestRig/DedicatedServer/CLAUDE.md,
# TestRig/ClientRig/README.md, both launchers' help text, the rig-lock reminder
# hook) points here. Change the rules here, nowhere else.
#
# ONE LOCK, BOTH HALVES
#   There is a single lock for the whole rig, held at TestRig/session.lock, and
#   it covers TestRig/DedicatedServer/ AND TestRig/ClientRig/ together. The two
#   halves are not independent resources:
#     - They share the developer's one Stationeers install (the client rig hard-
#       links out of it; the dedicated server mirrors BepInEx out of it).
#     - They share per-Windows-user Unity state that no setting separates:
#       %USERPROFILE%\AppData\LocalLow\Rocketwerkz\rocketstation\PlayerCookie-v2.xml
#       and the HKCU\Software\Rocketwerkz\rocketstation PlayerPrefs key.
#       The developer's own client shares them too.
#     - A multiplayer test uses both at once: the dedicated server hosts the
#       world, the client rig joins it.
#   Two separate locks would let one agent's client rig walk into another
#   agent's server session, and would make a multiplayer test acquire two locks
#   in an order that can deadlock against an agent taking them the other way.
#
#   Acquire from either launcher; the owner id works on both.
#       TestRig/DedicatedServer/dedicated-server.ps1 -Lock -Purpose "..."
#       TestRig/ClientRig/client-rig.ps1             -Lock -Purpose "..."
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS
#   One rig is shared by every agent on this machine. A test is many start/stop
#   cycles, so "is a process running" is not a good enough signal: between a
#   -Stop and the next -Start nothing runs, yet the session is still in
#   progress. This lock marks "a session owns the rig" across those gaps so a
#   second agent does not walk in and stomp the saves, the deployed mods, the
#   running world, or a provisioned client instance.
#
# HOW IT WORKS
#   - Acquire ONCE per session, before any mutating command:
#       <launcher> -Lock -Purpose "Playtesting <what> for <mod>"
#     It prints a short owner id. Pass that id as -As <id> on every mutating
#     command for the rest of the session, on EITHER launcher.
#   - Acquisition is SERIALISED across processes. Every read-modify-write of this
#     file runs inside a named system mutex, so two agents that both find the rig
#     free cannot both walk away believing they own it. Without that, the loser
#     would only discover it on its next mutating command, minutes and one full
#     provision later. The mutex is an implementation detail of
#     TestRig/rig-lock.ps1; nothing you type refers to it.
#   - Mutating actions on the dedicated server: -Bootstrap, -DeployMods,
#     -SyncMods, -Start, -Save, -SendCommand, -Stop. Read-only -Status and
#     -Logs never need it.
#   - Mutating actions on the client rig: -Provision, -Start, -Stop, -Save,
#     -Remove, -Broadcast, -Call. Read-only -Status, -List, -Logs and -Snapshot never
#     need it. -Wait is read-only but refreshes the lock when you already hold
#     it, because a barrier can run longer than the TTL.
#     -Broadcast and -Call are gated because they drive a LIVE client: /quit
#     ends it, and /savepath retargets where that client writes saves.
#   - Liveness: the lock is LIVE while the timer is fresh
#     (now - refreshed_at <= ttl_minutes) OR the rig is actually busy. Busy
#     means a player is connected to the running dedicated server, or at least
#     one client-rig instance process is alive. A live lock you do not own
#     blocks you. An expired lock with an idle rig is dead and is reclaimed
#     automatically.
#
# STATE HYGIENE HANGS OFF ACQUISITION (this is why it is here and not in a rule)
#   Taking a NEW lock RESETS the rig's between-session state, so a test cannot
#   fail because of what an unrelated test left behind. The lock is the only
#   mandatory choke point that already exists and is already enforced in code
#   (Assert-RigLockHeld refuses every mutating action without it), so an agent
#   cannot get the rig without getting it clean and cannot route around it. A
#   rule in a document is a rule an agent skips.
#
#   WHAT IT RESETS. Per client instance: setting.xml (it carries StartLocalHost,
#   and an instance that silently comes up hosting when the test believes it is a
#   joiner is the worst failure available here), data/<n>/userdata/saves/, the
#   Unity logs, imgui.ini, a STALE game.pid, the instance's BepInEx config
#   (re-copied from the source install, because POST /config/set persists by
#   default), LogOutput.log, the assembly cache, and the InspectorPlus requests/
#   and snapshots/ folders. On the dedicated server: the ScenarioRunner Scenario
#   value (blanked, file otherwise untouched), the scenariorunner requests/ and
#   give/ drop files, the InspectorPlus requests/ and snapshots/, data/setting.xml,
#   and stale server.pid / host.pid / control.cmd.
#
#   WHAT IT KEEPS. data/rig.json, every instance.json and provision.stamp, each
#   instance's seeded userdata/mods (a mod older than its source is REPORTED, and
#   the fix is -Provision -Force, not a delete), the dedicated server's saves and
#   mods and modconfig.xml, its other BepInEx configs (ones that moved since the
#   last reset are reported), and the ~1,050 hard links per instance.
#
#   SAFETY RULES ON THE RESET.
#     - It is REFUSED while the rig is in use: any live client instance, a live
#       dedicated server process, or an untracked game process. The lock is still
#       acquired and its owner id still printed, with a loud warning saying the
#       reset was skipped and why. An unclean rig must not become an unlockable
#       one.
#     - Re-asserting a lock you ALREADY hold never resets. An agent changing its
#       purpose or TTL mid-test would otherwise wipe its own run.
#     - -Lock -KeepState skips it on purpose, and prints exactly what it skipped.
#       Use it when a save, a config value or a scenario was staged deliberately.
#     - A failed reset is loud and names the instance, then throws. You hold the
#       lock; -Unlock and take it again to retry.
#
#   THE LIMIT, PLAINLY. This resets BETWEEN sessions, and a session deliberately
#   spans many start/stop cycles, so two unrelated tests run under ONE lock get
#   NO reset between them. If the next thing you test is unrelated to the last,
#   release the lock and take it again. Per-test hygiene would make the unit of
#   hygiene smaller than the unit of ownership, which is a lock-model change and
#   its own piece of work.
#
#   SHARED PER-USER STATE IS REPORTED, NEVER RESTORED. PlayerCookie-v2.xml, the
#   HKCU\Software\Rocketwerkz\rocketstation PlayerPrefs key and Blueprints\ cannot
#   be isolated (persistentDataPath is fixed inside globalgamemanagers), and
#   writing them back would itself be the write the save rules forbid. A cheap
#   snapshot is taken at acquisition into TestRig/session.state.json (gitignored),
#   and -Unlock / -Stop -Release print the delta. That converts "invisible until a
#   test fails" into "named at the session boundary", which is all it can honestly
#   do.
#
#   Implementation: TestRig/rig-reset.ps1, dot-sourced by both launchers next to
#   rig-lock.ps1. It also owns Set-RigSavePathOverride, which -Provision calls:
#   the config re-copy WIPES SavePathOverride, and an instance without it writes
#   its worlds into the developer's tier-1 save folder, so provision and reset
#   write that one setting through a single implementation.
#   Tests: TestRig/rig-reset.tests.ps1 (offline, temp directory, never the real
#   rig). Run it after any change there.
#
# QUEUEING FOR THE RIG (opt-in, bounded)
#   - By default, hitting another session's live lock FAILS IMMEDIATELY. That is
#     usually what you want: you find out at once and can tell the user.
#   - To wait instead:
#       <launcher> -Lock -Purpose "..." -WaitSeconds N
#     It polls until the rig frees up or N seconds elapse, printing the holder's
#     purpose while it waits, and then fails with the usual message.
#   - It is a QUEUE, not a reservation. No ordering fairness is promised: three
#     waiters do not get the rig in the order they arrived. Waiting is never
#     unbounded, and there is no way to make it unbounded.
#
# WHAT "BUSY" REPORTS, AND WHY THE WORDING MATTERS
#   The busy reason is what a human reads when deciding whether to authorize a
#   -BreakLock, so it names what is actually happening rather than a process
#   count: how many client instances are running, which one is HOSTING, and how
#   many clients are connected to that host. "2 client instance(s) running"
#   cannot tell a live hosted test at minute 40 from two instances somebody
#   forgot to stop; the current wording can.
#     - A pid file alone is NOT proof the rig is busy. Windows recycles process
#       ids and these pid files outlive their processes on a force-kill or a
#       reboot, so the launcher checks that the process really is the game before
#       counting it. Otherwise a recycled id would report busy forever and no
#       timer could ever reclaim the rig.
#     - An instance provisioned before the manifest carried a role is reported as
#       "role unknown". It still counts as busy; only the description degrades.
#     - UNTRACKED game processes (an orphan from a killed launcher or a crashed
#       test, claimed by no pid file) are reported loudly by -Status and in the
#       busy reason, but are deliberately NOT counted as busy. Nothing the
#       launchers do can stop an orphan, so counting it would pin the lock live
#       with no way out except -BreakLock. Kill them by pid instead.
#
# THE PURPOSE STRING
#   Keep it short and generic: "Playtesting network paint for SprayPaintPlus",
#   "Reproducing the auto-aim bug for PowerTransmitterPlus". It exists so the
#   user, when told another session holds the lock, can decide whether to wait,
#   take it, or leave it alone. Write it for that reader.
#
# REFRESH DISCIPLINE (this is what stops agents starving each other)
#   - The lock EXPIRES on a timer (ttl_minutes, default 10). That is deliberate:
#     an idle or finished agent must lose the lock so others can use the rig.
#   - Refresh ONLY while you are actively driving a test that needs the rig:
#       <launcher> -RefreshLock -As <id>
#     Every mutating command also refreshes it. A ~1 minute cadence during an
#     active loop is comfortable against a 10-minute TTL.
#   - NEVER refresh just to hold the rig for an absent human, and NEVER spawn
#     a background process whose only job is to refresh. Either one keeps the
#     lock alive after you are gone and starves every other agent. The refresh
#     must come from your own foreground actions.
#
# RELEASE WHEN DONE (client-rig sessions especially)
#   - Release explicitly: `-Unlock -As <id>`, or `-Stop -As <id> -Release`.
#   - A running client instance keeps the lock live on its own, with no timer to
#     save you. Leaving instances up after a test therefore holds the WHOLE rig,
#     dedicated server included, until somebody stops them. Always
#     `client-rig.ps1 -Stop -All -As <id>` before you release. `-Status` on
#     either launcher shows who holds the lock and why it is still live.
#   - TEAR THE HOST DOWN BEFORE YOU RELEASE. `-Unlock` REFUSES while a listen-host
#     instance is still live, because releasing hands the rig to the next agent
#     while a world is up and connected, and that agent's `-Stop -All` ends the
#     test with nothing left to say it happened. Stop the instances, then unlock.
#     `-Unlock -Force` overrides that one refusal when you really do mean to leave
#     a host running. `-Force` is NOT a lock breaker (see below): it cannot take
#     the lock off another session, only wave away a refusal inside your own.
#   - `-Stop -Release` frees the lock when it is yours, when you were authorized
#     to -BreakLock, or when it was already dead. The order of those two steps is
#     load bearing: -Stop asks for the lock STATE first, and that check is what
#     stops an unrelated -Stop -Release from freeing a busy session's expired
#     lock. Do not reorder them.
#
# WAITING FOR A HUMAN (e.g. "join and play, tell me when done")
#   - You are about to go idle, so you will NOT be refreshing. Tell the user the
#     reservation in plain terms, for example: "Rig reserved for about N
#     minutes. If you join, it stays held for as long as you are connected. If
#     nobody joins, it frees for other agents." Set -TtlMinutes to a sensible
#     join window when you hand off.
#   - While a player is actually connected the lock stays live on its own, no
#     refresh needed. If nobody joins, the timer lapses and the rig frees. That
#     is intended: an agent waiting on a sleeping user must not block the others.
#
# RESUMING AFTER A GAP (mandatory)
#   Time may have passed and your lock may be gone. BEFORE issuing any mutating
#   command on resume, run:
#       <launcher> -Status -As <id>
#   - Still yours -> -RefreshLock -As <id> and continue.
#   - Held by another session -> STOP. Do not touch the rig. Tell the user
#     what took it (the reported purpose) and that your reservation lapsed.
#     Re-acquire only on the user's go-ahead.
#
# BREAKING A LOCK IS HUMAN-GATED, AND HAS ITS OWN FLAG
#   -BreakLock breaks a LIVE lock held by another session (with -Lock /
#   -Unlock / -Stop). An agent may use it ONLY when the user explicitly
#   authorizes it in the conversation. Reclaiming an already-dead (expired,
#   rig idle) lock is automatic and is NOT a break.
#
#   It is NOT called -Force, on either launcher, and -Force never breaks a lock.
#   -Force means the same routine thing on both: override a refusal inside your
#   own session (client rig: `-Provision -Force` rebuilds an instance you
#   already own; either launcher: `-Unlock -Force` releases while one of your own
#   host instances is still up). Ownership is checked before any -Force refusal,
#   so -Force can never reach another session's lock. The two operations had
#   opposite risk under one flag name,
#   which is exactly the kind of collision that gets a live test torn down by
#   muscle memory. The dangerous one now has a name nobody types by accident.
#
# FIELDS (the active session.lock holds exactly these keys, one per line)
#   owner         short id printed by -Lock; echo it back with -As
#   purpose       short human-readable reason (see THE PURPOSE STRING)
#   acquired_at   ISO 8601 UTC, when first acquired
#   refreshed_at  ISO 8601 UTC, last refresh (drives the TTL)
#   ttl_minutes   inactivity window before the timer lapses (default 10)
#   host          machine name, for diagnostics
#
# TESTS
#   TestRig/rig-lock.tests.ps1 exercises all of the above offline: no game, no
#   instances, no network, against a temp directory, and it never touches the
#   real session.lock. It covers the state machine, the timer (including every
#   fail-closed case), ownership, -BreakLock, the busy signal, the file format,
#   the release ordering, and real concurrent processes racing for one lock.
#   TestRig/rig-reset.tests.ps1 does the same for the state reset: every reset
#   target, every preserved one, the SavePathOverride re-apply, the pid rules,
#   the busy refusal, the re-assert exemption, -KeepState and the drift report.
#   Run BOTH after ANY change to rig-lock.ps1 or rig-reset.ps1:
#       pwsh -NoProfile -File TestRig/rig-lock.tests.ps1
#       pwsh -NoProfile -File TestRig/rig-reset.tests.ps1
#   Each exits non-zero on failure and prints a pass/fail summary.
# =============================================================================

# Example values only. The real session.lock is written by the launchers via
# TestRig/rig-lock.ps1; do not hand-create it from this template.
owner=EXAMPLE0
purpose=Playtesting network paint for SprayPaintPlus
acquired_at=2026-01-01T00:00:00Z
refreshed_at=2026-01-01T00:00:00Z
ttl_minutes=10
host=EXAMPLE-PC
